WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.536 --> 00:00:05.005
(tense music)

2
00:00:33.768 --> 00:00:36.303
(music intensifies)

3
00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:03.430
(upbeat music)

4
00:01:16.243 --> 00:01:17.477
(car door opens)

5
00:01:20.247 --> 00:01:21.683
(car door closes)

6
00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:26.788
(door bell ringing)

7
00:01:27.689 --> 00:01:29.857
Mr. and Mrs. Gibbs,
I think I have someone here

8
00:01:29.891 --> 00:01:31.726
you might like to meet.

9
00:01:31.759 --> 00:01:33.728
Well, lookie here, Alice.

10
00:01:34.662 --> 00:01:36.898
[Alice] Oh, you must be Becky!

11
00:01:36.931 --> 00:01:38.833
Nice to meet you,
Mr. and Mrs. Gibbs.

12
00:01:38.866 --> 00:01:42.369
Oh, please call us
Ted and Alice.

13
00:01:42.402 --> 00:01:45.138
And this is Diego, right?

14
00:01:45.172 --> 00:01:46.841
That's right.

15
00:01:47.942 --> 00:01:49.343
Welcome home, honey.

16
00:01:49.376 --> 00:01:50.745
Welcome home.

17
00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:54.749
[Becky] <i>I know what</i>
<i>you're thinking.</i>

18
00:01:54.782 --> 00:01:56.684
<i>"Who the fuck is this girl?</i>

19
00:01:56.718 --> 00:01:59.252
<i>That is not the Becky</i>
<i>I remember."</i>

20
00:01:59.286 --> 00:02:02.824
<i>But I had to play the part</i>
<i>for my new foster parents.</i>

21
00:02:02.857 --> 00:02:05.158
<i>Yeah, these two wackjobs.</i>

22
00:02:05.192 --> 00:02:07.327
(upbeat music)

23
00:02:10.197 --> 00:02:11.799
[Ted] You know,
when Alice and I decided

24
00:02:11.833 --> 00:02:13.367
we were gonna be foster parents,

25
00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.369
we knew we had to move
to a community

26
00:02:15.402 --> 00:02:18.806
with the best of the best
in Christ-filled education

27
00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:22.910
and around here they're all
blue ribbon school districts.

28
00:02:22.944 --> 00:02:26.781
Top tier, top of their class,
and top notch.

29
00:02:26.814 --> 00:02:29.349
Well, I'm not sure what all
is planned for tomorrow

30
00:02:29.383 --> 00:02:31.786
but I would love
to get enrolled ASAP.

31
00:02:31.819 --> 00:02:34.388
Absolutely.
I planned on taking you there

32
00:02:34.421 --> 00:02:35.757
first thing in the morning.

33
00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:36.824
Wonderful.

34
00:02:36.858 --> 00:02:38.593
Oh, oh, and don't forget about
the new principal.

35
00:02:38.626 --> 00:02:40.260
-[Alice] Mr. Serio.
-[Ted] Good guy.

36
00:02:40.293 --> 00:02:41.729
We've gone to the same church
now for what?

37
00:02:41.763 --> 00:02:42.764
[Ted] Six years!

38
00:02:42.797 --> 00:02:44.599
And he has a daughter
that's your age, Sarah.

39
00:02:44.632 --> 00:02:45.399
She's adorable.

40
00:02:45.432 --> 00:02:46.601
She'll probably
be in your class.

41
00:02:46.634 --> 00:02:47.835
She is in a wheelchair

42
00:02:47.869 --> 00:02:50.203
but you'll still like her
anyway.

43
00:02:50.237 --> 00:02:50.872
[Becky] Well, will I get
to spend time

44
00:02:50.905 --> 00:02:51.939
with her in church as well?

45
00:02:51.973 --> 00:02:54.809
Perhaps there's like
a youth group of some sort.

46
00:02:54.842 --> 00:02:56.711
-[Alice] Oh, Becky.
-Wow.

47
00:02:56.744 --> 00:02:58.813
I can't tell you
how happy it makes me

48
00:02:58.846 --> 00:03:00.414
to hear you say that.

49
00:03:00.447 --> 00:03:01.916
Well, shall we?

50
00:03:01.949 --> 00:03:04.819
[Becky] Yes, um, it's just...

51
00:03:04.852 --> 00:03:06.954
would you mind
if we said grace first?

52
00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:10.323
(Ted sighs)
(Alice chuckles)

53
00:03:10.357 --> 00:03:11.959
-Can you believe it?
-She's perfect.

54
00:03:12.492 --> 00:03:14.227
(gentle music)

55
00:03:15.596 --> 00:03:18.533
And just remember,
if you need anything at all,

56
00:03:18.566 --> 00:03:20.702
Ted and I are just
in the next room.

57
00:03:20.735 --> 00:03:23.538
My guardian angels.

58
00:03:25.707 --> 00:03:27.474
(owl hooting)

59
00:03:30.745 --> 00:03:32.547
Goodnight, sweetheart.

60
00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.381
Goodnight, Mom.

61
00:03:34.414 --> 00:03:35.683
(sighs)

62
00:03:36.017 --> 00:03:38.019
Ted!
Teddy!

63
00:03:38.052 --> 00:03:39.286
(door closes)

64
00:03:40.353 --> 00:03:42.222
(intense music)

65
00:03:43.991 --> 00:03:45.425
(window slams)

66
00:03:49.664 --> 00:03:51.766
Diego, come.

67
00:03:51.799 --> 00:03:53.968
(intense music continues)

68
00:04:51.859 --> 00:04:53.861
(intense music continues)

69
00:05:38.606 --> 00:05:39.807
(birds chirping)

70
00:05:40.942 --> 00:05:43.511
[Becky] <i>It's been two years</i>
<i>since four Neo-Nazis</i>

71
00:05:43.544 --> 00:05:46.681
<i>invaded my family's lake house</i>
<i>and murdered my father.</i>

72
00:05:46.714 --> 00:05:48.783
<i>They were looking</i>
<i>for some stupid fucking key</i>

73
00:05:48.816 --> 00:05:51.484
<i>that they had hidden</i>
<i>below the house.</i>

74
00:05:51.519 --> 00:05:52.720
<i>Yeah.</i>

75
00:05:52.753 --> 00:05:55.723
<i>That stupid fucking key.</i>

76
00:05:55.756 --> 00:05:57.792
<i>But then, this happened.</i>

77
00:05:57.825 --> 00:05:59.093
(crashing)
(gasping)

78
00:05:59.126 --> 00:06:00.094
(engine cranking)
(gurgling)

79
00:06:00.127 --> 00:06:00.862
(screaming)

80
00:06:00.895 --> 00:06:01.829
(gunshot)

81
00:06:01.863 --> 00:06:02.930
(screaming)

82
00:06:03.998 --> 00:06:06.567
<i>Bummer about me</i>
<i>ruining their plans.</i>

83
00:06:06.601 --> 00:06:08.803
(tense music)

84
00:06:09.604 --> 00:06:10.671
<i>I'm sixteen now.</i>

85
00:06:10.705 --> 00:06:13.174
<i>I've run away from</i>
<i>three foster homes.</i>

86
00:06:13.207 --> 00:06:16.978
<i>I've manipulated,</i>
<i>conned, burglarized,</i>

87
00:06:17.011 --> 00:06:18.913
<i>anything to keep me</i>
<i>off the grid</i>

88
00:06:18.946 --> 00:06:20.615
<i>and out of the system.</i>

89
00:06:20.648 --> 00:06:23.084
<i>If you ask me,</i>
<i>given the circumstances,</i>

90
00:06:24.752 --> 00:06:26.687
<i>I think I'm doing just fine.</i>

91
00:06:26.721 --> 00:06:28.990
(upbeat music)

92
00:06:33.227 --> 00:06:34.929
(grunting)

93
00:06:45.006 --> 00:06:46.841
(upbeat music continues)
(birds chirping)

94
00:06:52.947 --> 00:06:54.882
(grunting)

95
00:06:57.618 --> 00:07:01.454
<i>Oh yeah, I fall into</i>
<i>my own hole traps sometimes.</i>

96
00:07:01.488 --> 00:07:03.456
<i>Nobody's perfect.</i>

97
00:07:08.062 --> 00:07:10.064
(gentle music)

98
00:07:18.973 --> 00:07:20.608
[Becky] Eat.

99
00:07:20.641 --> 00:07:22.777
Thank you, darlin'.

100
00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:24.211
[Becky] <i>This is Elena Cahn.</i>

101
00:07:24.245 --> 00:07:26.847
<i>I've lived with her</i>
<i>for the past year.</i>

102
00:07:26.881 --> 00:07:28.115
<i>I don't know much about her</i>

103
00:07:28.149 --> 00:07:29.951
<i>other than the following:</i>

104
00:07:29.984 --> 00:07:33.254
<i>She's old,</i>
<i>she hates almost everyone,</i>

105
00:07:33.287 --> 00:07:35.556
<i>and she picks up hitchhikers.</i>

106
00:07:35.589 --> 00:07:36.991
<i>It's how we met.</i>

107
00:07:37.024 --> 00:07:39.627
I fell into one
of my hole traps again today.

108
00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:41.562
I saw that.

109
00:07:41.595 --> 00:07:43.531
I had just woken up,

110
00:07:43.564 --> 00:07:45.533
looked out my window

111
00:07:45.566 --> 00:07:48.269
just in time to see you vanish.

112
00:07:48.302 --> 00:07:50.972
I quite enjoyed the show.

113
00:07:55.643 --> 00:07:56.711
You sleep?

114
00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:00.681
No.

115
00:08:00.715 --> 00:08:02.149
(gentle music)
(crickets chirping)

116
00:08:02.183 --> 00:08:04.719
<i>It's the same every night.</i>

117
00:08:14.962 --> 00:08:15.930
(muffled gunshot)

118
00:08:16.597 --> 00:08:18.099
(not audible)

119
00:08:19.633 --> 00:08:20.868
What about you?

120
00:08:22.003 --> 00:08:23.804
Like a baby.

121
00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:27.908
Well?

122
00:08:28.876 --> 00:08:30.277
I always start.

123
00:08:30.311 --> 00:08:32.813
(scoffs) So what's
different about today?

124
00:08:34.782 --> 00:08:37.018
I am grateful for food.

125
00:08:40.054 --> 00:08:42.923
I am grateful
for a hot cup of coffee.

126
00:08:42.957 --> 00:08:45.960
How come I can't say food
but you can say coffee?

127
00:08:45.993 --> 00:08:49.030
Food is a human necessity.

128
00:08:49.063 --> 00:08:52.166
Coffee is a delicacy.

129
00:08:54.802 --> 00:08:57.738
I am grateful that
I get paid today.

130
00:08:57.772 --> 00:08:58.639
Hmm.

131
00:09:00.674 --> 00:09:04.145
I am grateful
for the novel I'm reading.

132
00:09:04.178 --> 00:09:06.180
What's it called?

133
00:09:06.213 --> 00:09:08.649
True Grit.

134
00:09:08.682 --> 00:09:10.618
I think you'd like it.

135
00:09:12.053 --> 00:09:15.089
I am grateful for Diego.

136
00:09:15.122 --> 00:09:17.558
Hm. He's a good boy.

137
00:09:18.859 --> 00:09:19.927
One more.

138
00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:21.629
Wow me.

139
00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:26.634
I am grateful...for...

140
00:09:26.667 --> 00:09:28.569
Another day.

141
00:09:29.270 --> 00:09:30.771
...another day.

142
00:09:30.805 --> 00:09:32.173
You know, there should
really be a rule against you

143
00:09:32.206 --> 00:09:34.208
always saying that
as your number three.

144
00:09:34.241 --> 00:09:35.776
You only have to think
of two things

145
00:09:35.810 --> 00:09:37.378
if it's always
your number three.

146
00:09:37.411 --> 00:09:39.914
I get to change the rules.

147
00:09:39.947 --> 00:09:41.115
I'm old.

148
00:09:42.049 --> 00:09:44.218
(upbeat doo-wop music)

149
00:09:48.956 --> 00:09:51.859
Founding Fathers Breakfast
with a side of toast.

150
00:09:51.892 --> 00:09:53.894
Thanks, sweetheart.

151
00:09:56.197 --> 00:09:57.865
Do you see my name tag?

152
00:09:57.898 --> 00:10:00.134
-I'm sorry?
-My name tag.

153
00:10:00.167 --> 00:10:02.103
Does it say "Sweetheart"?

154
00:10:02.136 --> 00:10:03.671
No.

155
00:10:03.704 --> 00:10:05.673
What does it say?

156
00:10:05.706 --> 00:10:06.941
Becky.

157
00:10:06.974 --> 00:10:09.910
Yeah, 'cause that's my name.

158
00:10:09.944 --> 00:10:12.680
Okay, Jesus.

159
00:10:12.713 --> 00:10:15.616
Oh, sweetheart--Becky...

160
00:10:16.317 --> 00:10:18.252
I asked for buttered toast.

161
00:10:18.285 --> 00:10:20.187
There's butter on the plate.

162
00:10:20.221 --> 00:10:23.724
Yeah, but...I like the butter
to be melted into the toast

163
00:10:23.757 --> 00:10:26.827
the minute it pops
out of the toaster.

164
00:10:27.928 --> 00:10:29.130
(plate breaking)
(doo-wop music swells)

165
00:10:29.163 --> 00:10:30.631
(slashing)

166
00:10:33.767 --> 00:10:35.136
(gurgling)

167
00:10:36.370 --> 00:10:37.805
(giggling)

168
00:10:37.838 --> 00:10:39.273
(gurgling)

169
00:10:40.474 --> 00:10:42.176
(laughing)

170
00:10:43.310 --> 00:10:45.279
[Becky] <i>I didn't actually do it.</i>

171
00:10:45.312 --> 00:10:46.947
[Man] Hello?

172
00:10:46.981 --> 00:10:50.684
<i>But I really, really wanted to.</i>

173
00:11:01.996 --> 00:11:03.364
Shit.

174
00:11:08.335 --> 00:11:11.105
(clock ticking)
(intense music)

175
00:11:19.180 --> 00:11:20.447
(clock punches)

176
00:11:26.954 --> 00:11:29.123
[Radio] <i>A Jasper Hills man</i>
<i>is now facing</i>

177
00:11:29.156 --> 00:11:30.858
<i>first degree murder charges.</i>

178
00:11:30.891 --> 00:11:37.231
[Becky] K-I-L-L-E-R.

179
00:11:38.299 --> 00:11:42.069
Double E, Double Word,
twenty-two.

180
00:11:42.102 --> 00:11:43.837
(crickets chirping)

181
00:11:44.138 --> 00:11:47.241
You can tell a lot
about a person,

182
00:11:47.274 --> 00:11:49.777
playing a game like this.

183
00:11:52.246 --> 00:11:54.014
How so?

184
00:11:54.048 --> 00:11:56.784
By the words they're able
to find within their letters.

185
00:11:59.920 --> 00:12:02.356
My brother made every effort

186
00:12:02.389 --> 00:12:05.092
to play the most ostentatious
of words.

187
00:12:05.125 --> 00:12:08.429
He was a real
self-righteous jackass.

188
00:12:08.462 --> 00:12:11.465
Two Triple Letters,
ten points.

189
00:12:14.802 --> 00:12:16.136
I didn't know
you had a brother.

190
00:12:16.170 --> 00:12:18.506
Four brothers,
all older.

191
00:12:18.540 --> 00:12:20.341
Do you still talk to them?

192
00:12:22.142 --> 00:12:24.178
Only one is still alive.

193
00:12:26.046 --> 00:12:28.015
The jackass.

194
00:12:30.518 --> 00:12:32.820
I'm sorry.

195
00:12:32.853 --> 00:12:34.255
You didn't kill 'em.

196
00:12:34.288 --> 00:12:35.889
Besides, I've learned that

197
00:12:35.923 --> 00:12:37.925
for every person
you lose along the way,

198
00:12:37.958 --> 00:12:40.227
you gain another.

199
00:12:40.261 --> 00:12:42.296
(gentle music)
(crickets chirping)

200
00:13:00.180 --> 00:13:01.348
Anew.

201
00:13:03.117 --> 00:13:04.385
Ten.

202
00:13:08.523 --> 00:13:11.992
Why have you never asked me
where I came from?

203
00:13:12.026 --> 00:13:14.161
Why have you never told me?

204
00:13:14.194 --> 00:13:15.896
Weren't you curious?

205
00:13:15.929 --> 00:13:17.865
A kid with no parents
showed up

206
00:13:17.898 --> 00:13:19.800
wanting to rent a room.

207
00:13:20.635 --> 00:13:22.369
You pay your way.

208
00:13:22.403 --> 00:13:24.972
You help out around the house.

209
00:13:25.005 --> 00:13:27.575
And I'd hardly consider
you a child.

210
00:13:27.609 --> 00:13:30.311
You are eighteen, right?

211
00:13:34.982 --> 00:13:37.985
My mother died
when I was nine.

212
00:13:38.018 --> 00:13:39.853
My father was a drunk.

213
00:13:39.887 --> 00:13:42.156
I left home when I was fifteen.

214
00:13:42.189 --> 00:13:45.292
We all come from somewhere.

215
00:13:45.326 --> 00:13:48.462
And we all do
what we have to...

216
00:13:52.266 --> 00:13:54.335
...in order to survive.

217
00:13:56.638 --> 00:13:58.606
How much does that add up to?

218
00:14:01.141 --> 00:14:02.076
[Becky] <i>Fifteen.</i>

219
00:14:02.109 --> 00:14:03.177
Ten.

220
00:14:09.350 --> 00:14:10.384
(chuckles)

221
00:14:10.417 --> 00:14:12.520
Nice try.

222
00:14:12.554 --> 00:14:14.455
[Radio] <i>We go now to</i>
<i>a developing story.</i>

223
00:14:14.488 --> 00:14:16.323
<i>They call themselves</i>
<i>the Noble Men</i>

224
00:14:16.357 --> 00:14:18.325
<i>and they may be coming</i>
<i>here to Fillmore.</i>

225
00:14:18.359 --> 00:14:19.460
<i>Authorities are aware</i>

226
00:14:19.493 --> 00:14:20.994
<i>of the risk of violence</i>
<i>at this week's--</i>

227
00:14:21.028 --> 00:14:22.963
The world has gone mad.

228
00:14:22.996 --> 00:14:23.997
<i>...increasing</i>
<i>the security detail</i>

229
00:14:24.031 --> 00:14:26.133
<i>for Senator Anne Hernandez.</i>

230
00:14:26.166 --> 00:14:27.901
<i>They say they've been tracking</i>
<i>the Noble Men</i>

231
00:14:27.935 --> 00:14:30.137
<i>on various online platforms</i>
<i>for months now.</i>

232
00:14:30.170 --> 00:14:31.438
<i>They have seen a rise</i>
<i>in activity</i>

233
00:14:31.472 --> 00:14:33.307
<i>and they've noticed</i>
<i>a pattern of their appearances</i>

234
00:14:33.340 --> 00:14:34.709
<i>within smaller communities.</i>

235
00:14:34.743 --> 00:14:37.044
<i>Whether or not the Noble Men</i>
<i>decide to make an appearance</i>

236
00:14:37.077 --> 00:14:38.912
<i>at the town hall</i>
<i>in Fillmore on Wednesday</i>

237
00:14:38.946 --> 00:14:40.447
<i>and what that appearance</i>
<i>may look like</i>

238
00:14:40.481 --> 00:14:43.050
<i>only time will tell.</i>

239
00:14:43.083 --> 00:14:45.219
(intense music)

240
00:14:58.399 --> 00:15:00.167
(bell ringing)

241
00:15:06.306 --> 00:15:07.675
(man clears throat)

242
00:15:11.311 --> 00:15:13.113
So are you gonna seat us
or you just gonna stand there?

243
00:15:13.147 --> 00:15:15.115
Yeah, wait, real quick,
I got a question.

244
00:15:15.149 --> 00:15:18.085
Um, did your mom
actually name you that?

245
00:15:18.118 --> 00:15:19.987
'Cause Becky is a stripper name

246
00:15:20.020 --> 00:15:22.524
and any chick that gives
her daughter a stripper name

247
00:15:22.557 --> 00:15:24.191
is a catch.

248
00:15:24.224 --> 00:15:25.727
What is it with you and moms?

249
00:15:25.760 --> 00:15:28.395
-I got a thing for moms.
-No, what he's saying is

250
00:15:28.429 --> 00:15:29.531
he'd love to fuck your mom.

251
00:15:29.564 --> 00:15:33.000
That is correct, yes,
I would love to fuck your mom.

252
00:15:34.468 --> 00:15:36.103
Right this way.

253
00:15:36.671 --> 00:15:37.739
Not a fan.

254
00:15:37.772 --> 00:15:38.573
Come on.

255
00:15:38.606 --> 00:15:39.574
(grinding)

256
